{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>unto Eleazar the priest</b> – its mitzvah is in the deputy [segan], in the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 19:3). But the intent is not to say that the obligation of the mitzvah falls upon the deputy more than upon the High Priest or an ordinary priest; rather, it was a one-time ruling for the first cow that it be done through Eleazar, and he was the deputy. Thus the language of Sifrei (Chukat 8): the verse comes and teaches about the cow that it must be performed by the deputy. Know that this is so, for Aaron was alive yet Eleazar slaughtered the cow. \"And you shall give it unto Eleazar the priest\" — this one was performed by Eleazar, and all the other cows by the High Priest — the words of Rabbi Meir. Rabbi Yehose and Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi Shimon and Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov say: this one was performed by Eleazar, and all the other cows whether by a High Priest or by an ordinary priest.\n\nThe meaning of the matter is to say that this mitzvah, in the depth of its secret, is worthy of being given to the greatest of the priesthood. And it was not given to Aaron — perhaps for his greatness, for he is the holy one of HaShem and His devout one who makes atonement in His sanctuary, and He did not wish to give him outside service. Or it was to crown Eleazar and train him during his father's lifetime in one of the mitzvot of the high priesthood, or it was as a punishment for the calf, according to the words of Rabbi Moshe ha-Darshan. And behold, the Holy One, blessed be He, made Aaron too a prophet in this mitzvah, and he and Moses our teacher would give it to Eleazar, who is the head of the priesthood after Aaron, and he is the one anointed with the anointing oil. And this is a hint for the generations that it be performed by the head of the priesthood, namely the High Priest. And thus we learned (Parah 4:1): a sin-offering cow whose slaughter was not done by a High Priest is invalid, and Rabbi Yehudah declares it valid. And in the Gemara in tractate Yoma (Yoma 42a): \"And you shall give it unto Eleazar the priest\" — it, unto Eleazar, but for the generations, not unto Eleazar [i.e. not necessarily a deputy]. There are those who say: for the generations, by an ordinary priest; and there are those who say: for the generations, by a High Priest. And this is the anonymous text of our Mishnah and is the correct understanding in the reasoning. And according to the one who says \"for the generations by an ordinary priest,\" He commanded through Eleazar because there was no other priest there, for it is not fitting that his younger brother should precede him."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The citation from Yoma is identified as Yoma 42a based on the standard pagination; the Hebrew text gives only 'מ״ב' (42)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:07:00.612802995Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}